Stokes law assumes that the ball was dropped in a vast uniform ocean of glycerin. Because our beaker has a finite radius the walls of the beaker slightly slow the ball down. In a small graduated cylinder they would have slowed the ball down much more. Below there is a graph of the wall correction factor, the factor you need to divide the measured viscosity by to get the correct viscosity.}

